"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkmy_blog=globalThis.webpackChunkmy_blog||[]).push([[9724],{6048(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"bai-viet-thu-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/bai-viet-thu-2","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/Cpython.md","source":"@site/blog/Cpython.md","title":"What is CPython?","description":"In this article, I will share what I know from learning about CPython.","date":"2026-02-02T11:20:01.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docusaurus","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/docusaurus","description":"Docusaurus tag description"},{"inline":true,"label":"blog","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"huong-dan","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/huong-dan"},{"inline":true,"label":"lap-trinh","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/lap-trinh"}],"readingTime":4.08,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Pham Trong Nghia","title":"Administrator","url":"https://github.com/trongnghia1811","imageURL":"https://github.com/trongnghia1811.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"bai-viet-thu-2","title":"What is CPython?","authors":["admin"],"tags":["docusaurus","blog","huong-dan","lap-trinh"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Guide to creating a personal blog using Docusaurus","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/bai-viet-dau-tien"}},"content":"In this article, I will share what I know from learning about CPython.\\r\\n\\r\\n- What is CPython?\\r\\n- How does the journey from a line of code in the IDE to execution happen?\\r\\n- How does CPython manage memory?\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. What is CPython?\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore going into detail, we need to clarify: Python is a language (a set of grammatical rules). CPython is the software that executes those rules (Implementation).\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen we download Python from the homepage, we are actually downloading CPython. It is written entirely in C. It acts as both a Compiler and an Interpreter.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. The Journey of Code: Pipeline from A to Z\\r\\n\\r\\nConsider the following example:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\na = 10\\r\\nprint(a + 5)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nFor the computer to understand, this line of code must go through a strict process consisting of 4 stages:\\r\\n\\r\\n### Stage 1: Lexing (Lexical Analysis)\\r\\n\\r\\nCPython reads our code file as a meaningless string of text. The Lexer will chop that string into meaningful units called Tokens.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\na -> NAME\\r\\n= -> OP\\r\\n10 -> NUMBER\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nPython\'s Lexer is very special: it also cares about whitespace (indentation) to generate INDENT and DEDENT tokens, helping to shape the code blocks.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Stage 2: Parsing & AST (Building the Abstract Syntax Tree)\\r\\n\\r\\nFrom the discrete tokens above, the Parser will arrange them according to the grammar and build an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). The computer doesn\'t read from left to right like humans; it looks at the tree structure to know which is the parent, which is the child, and which has execution priority (e.g., multiplication and division first, addition and subtraction later).\\r\\n\\r\\n### Stage 3: Compilation (Compiling to Bytecode)\\r\\n\\r\\nAt this point, an extremely important step takes place: CPython is forced to translate the AST into an intermediate language called Bytecode (.pyc).\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Note**: Python does not directly run the source code (.py) line by line as many people mistakenly believe.\\r\\n\\r\\n- It always has to Compile to Bytecode first. This Bytecode is not Machine Code for the CPU to run, but an instruction set specifically for the Python virtual machine.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Giai \u0111o\u1ea1n 4: Python Virtual Machine (PVM)\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is where the program actually runs. The PVM is an infinite loop that acts as a \\"virtual CPU\\".\\r\\nWith the statement a = 10, the memory model operates entirely differently from traditional C/Java:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Heap Memory (Contains Objects): The PVM creates an int object with a value of 10 located on the Heap.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Stack Memory (Contains References): The PVM pushes the variable name a (reference) onto the current Stack frame and points it to the address of the object 10 on the Heap.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Key difference**: The Stack in Python does not contain \\"values\\"; it only contains references. Everything in Python (numbers, strings, booleans...) is an Object and resides on the Heap.\\r\\nFinally, the PVM reads each line of Bytecode and calls the corresponding C functions for the CPU to execute.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. How does CPython manage memory?\\r\\n\\r\\nCPython manages memory extremely sophisticatedly.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Everything is an Object (PyObject)\\r\\n\\r\\nIn CPython, the number 10 is not just 4 binary bytes. It is a C struct named PyObject. It contains a lot of metadata:\\r\\n\\r\\n- What type is it (int, str)?\\r\\n- How many references are currently using it (Reference Count)?\\r\\n\\r\\n### Pymalloc & Allocation Mechanism\\r\\n\\r\\nCPython does not call malloc (requesting RAM from the OS) every time we create a variable, because that is very slow. Instead, it uses Pymalloc:\\r\\n\\r\\n- It pre-allocates large chunks of memory (Arenas).\\r\\n- Subdivides them into pools (Pools).\\r\\n- When we need it, it immediately \\"issues\\" a small block. This is why creating small objects in Python is very fast.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Garbage Collection\\r\\n\\r\\nHow does Python know when to delete a variable to free up RAM?\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Reference Counting (Primary)**: Each object has a counter. Whenever a variable references it, the count is +1; when it\'s no longer used (or del is used), it\'s -1. When the counter reaches 0, the object is deleted immediately.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Generational Garbage Collection (Handling Cyclic References):**\\r\\n\\r\\n- Problem: If A points to B, and B points back to A (Reference Cycle), the Reference Count of both will never reach 0, even if we no longer use them. This is a memory leak.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Solution: CPython uses a Generational Garbage Collector. It periodically scans \\"container\\" objects (lists, dicts, classes...) to detect these closed cycles and clean them up.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n##\\r\\n\\r\\nYou can refer to the slide below for a deeper understanding of CPython:\\r\\n\\r\\n- \ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAG_3EC7Suw/1LjWwYMgO59FCa64VBrI3w/edit\\r\\n  (The slide will open in a new tab for a fuller and clearer view)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Conclusion\\r\\n\\r\\nThe journey from a line of code in the IDE to when the OS executes it is a long path through the Lexer, Parser, Compiler, and PVM. CPython is not just an interpreter; it is an engineering masterpiece that combines the flexibility of a high-level language with the strictness of C.\\r\\n\\r\\nHopefully, this article gives you a deeper insight into the tool you use every day. Don\'t just code; understand how the code runs!"},{"id":"bai-viet-dau-tien","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/bai-viet-dau-tien","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/baivietdautien.md","source":"@site/blog/baivietdautien.md","title":"Guide to creating a personal blog using Docusaurus","description":"In this article, I will guide you in detail on how to create a personal blog using Docusaurus \u2013 a very powerful tool for building static documentation websites and blogs.","date":"2026-01-22T16:33:26.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docusaurus","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/docusaurus","description":"Docusaurus tag description"},{"inline":true,"label":"blog","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"huong-dan","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/huong-dan"},{"inline":true,"label":"lap-trinh","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/lap-trinh"}],"readingTime":0.91,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Pham Trong Nghia","title":"Administrator","url":"https://github.com/trongnghia1811","imageURL":"https://github.com/trongnghia1811.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"bai-viet-dau-tien","title":"Guide to creating a personal blog using Docusaurus","authors":["admin"],"tags":["docusaurus","blog","huong-dan","lap-trinh"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"What is CPython?","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/bai-viet-thu-2"}},"content":"In this article, I will guide you in detail on how to create a personal blog using _Docusaurus_ \u2013 a very powerful tool for building static documentation websites and blogs.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis article is suitable for:\\r\\n\\r\\n- IT students\\r\\n- Web beginners\\r\\n- People who want a professional personal blog\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. What is Docusaurus?\\r\\n\\r\\nDocusaurus is an open-source framework developed by Facebook, used to create:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Personal blogs\\r\\n- Documentation websites\\r\\n- Project introduction websites\\r\\n\\r\\nAdvantages:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Free\\r\\n- Easy to use\\r\\n- Write posts in Markdown\\r\\n- Easy to deploy to GitHub Pages\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Environment preparation\\r\\n\\r\\nYou need to have installed:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Node.js (version >= 18)\\r\\n- npm (comes with Node.js)\\r\\n\\r\\nCheck with the commands:\\r\\n\\r\\nnode -v\\r\\nnpm -v\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n##\\r\\n\\r\\nThe slide below describes the entire website building process, from initializing the project to deploying the website to GitHub Pages.\\r\\n\\r\\nView the detailed guide slide here:\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAG-x-HN2J0/AXmxIe_VDDip6aalEPP8PQ/view\\r\\n\\r\\n(The slide will open in a new tab for a fuller and clearer view)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Conclusion\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter viewing the slide, beginners can:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Create a Docusaurus project\\r\\n- Write a blog post\\r\\n- Push source code to GitHub\\r\\n- Deploy the website using GitHub Pages\\r\\n\\r\\n---"}]}}')}}]);